          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "iterative is the lowest-area family (single datapath, no pipeline registers). It produces 1 result every N+3 cycles, so to meet >=1 MSPS it needs Fmax >= N+3 MHz. With N in 10..16 that is only ~13..19 MHz, which is trivially achievable in sky130hd, so the throughput constraint is not binding for this family. The real trade-off is accuracy vs area: max_abs_err <= 2^-10 requires enough n_iter and data_width. I bound data_width to 12..16 (W=12 gives Q1.10 output, the minimum to even represent 2^-10 error; W=16 gives headroom for accuracy_bits objective) and n_iter to 10..16 (N=10 is near the minimum for 2^-10 accuracy with modest guards; N=16 gives high accuracy_bits at the cost of more area). angle_guard 0..2 and frac_guard 0..2 cover the accuracy-relevant range without wasting bits on area. rounding both options to let the optimizer find the cheapest accurate variant."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me. iterative is strictly lower area than unrolled_k for the same N and W (unrolled_k duplicates datapath logic k times), and since the throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) is easily met by iterative at low Fmax, iterative should dominate the Pareto front on area. I therefore spend the entire budget on iterative, focusing the search on the accuracy-vs-area trade-off: data_width 12..16 and n_iter 10..16 with small guards. This range is tight enough to let Optuna resolve the front within 100 evals. unrolled_k is omitted because it cannot beat iterative on area for this spec (throughput is not the bottleneck), and spending budget there would dilute the search on the family that actually defines the front."
}
